How many paths are there from $A$ to $B$ along the grid lines shown, moving only to the right or up at each step, that do not pass through the point $P$? Two paths are different if they differ in at least one segment.

[asy]
size(200);
for(int i=0; i<=7; ++i) draw((i,0)--(i,5), gray(0.7)+linewidth(0.5));
for(int j=0; j<=5; ++j) draw((0,j)--(7,j), gray(0.7)+linewidth(0.5));
draw((0,0)--(7,0)--(7,5)--(0,5)--cycle, linewidth(1));
for(int i=0; i<=7; ++i) label("$"+string(i)+"$",(i,-0.4),S);
for(int j=0; j<=5; ++j) label("$"+string(j)+"$",(-0.4,j),W);
dot((0,0)); dot((7,5)); dot((3,2), red);
label("$A$",(0,0),SW);
label("$B$",(7,5),NE);
label("$P$",(3,2),SE);
[/asy]

answer:
Every path from $A$ to $B$ consists of $7$ steps to the right and $5$ steps up in some order, so without any restriction there are $\binom{12}{5}=792$ paths. We subtract the paths that pass through $P$.

[asy]
size(120);
for(int i=0; i<=7; ++i) draw((i,0)--(i,5), gray);
for(int j=0; j<=5; ++j) draw((0,j)--(7,j), gray);
draw((0,0)--(3,0)--(3,2)--(7,2)--(7,5), linewidth(1.5));
label("$A$",(0,0),SW); label("$P$",(3,2),NW); label("$B$",(7,5),NE);
[/asy]

A path through $P$ consists of a path from $A$ to $P$, which uses $3$ steps right and $2$ steps up, so there are $\binom{5}{2}=10$ of these, followed by a path from $P$ to $B$, which uses $4$ steps right and $3$ steps up, so there are $\binom{7}{3}=35$ of these. Thus $10\cdot35=350$ paths pass through $P$, and the number of paths that avoid $P$ is $792-350=\boxed{442}$.

