answer:
We enclose the triangle in the rectangle with corners $(-5,-3)$, $(9,-3)$, $(9,8)$, and $(-5,8)$, which has width $14$ and height $11$.

[asy]
size(130);
draw((-5,-3)--(9,-3)--(9,8)--(-5,8)--cycle, dashed);
draw((2,-3)--(9,4)--(-5,8)--cycle, linewidth(1));
label("$A$",(2,-3),S); label("$B$",(9,4),E); label("$C$",(-5,8),NW);
[/asy]

The rectangle has area $14\cdot11=154$. The three right triangles in the corners, outside $\triangle ABC$, have areas
\[\frac12\cdot7\cdot7=\frac{49}{2},\qquad\frac12\cdot14\cdot4=28,\qquad\frac12\cdot7\cdot11=\frac{77}{2}.\]
Their total is $\frac{49}{2}+28+\frac{77}{2}=91$. Therefore the area of $\triangle ABC$ is $154-91=\boxed{63}$.

