The regular hexagon shown has side length $6$. What is the area of the equilateral triangle whose vertices are three alternate vertices of the hexagon? Express your answer in simplest radical form.

[asy]
size(110);
pair[] P;
for(int i=0; i<6; ++i) P[i]=6*dir(60*i);
draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--P[3]--P[4]--P[5]--cycle);
draw(P[0]--P[2]--P[4]--cycle);
[/asy]

answer:
Each corner of the hexagon cut off by the triangle is itself a triangle with two sides of length $6$ meeting at an angle of $120^\circ$.

[asy]
size(110);
pair[] P;
for(int i=0; i<6; ++i) P[i]=6*dir(60*i);
draw(P[0]--P[1]--P[2]--P[3]--P[4]--P[5]--cycle);
draw(P[0]--P[2]--P[4]--cycle);
label("$6$",(P[0]+P[1])/2,NE); label("$6$",(P[1]+P[2])/2,N);
[/asy]

Each such corner triangle has area $\frac12\cdot6\cdot6\cdot\sin120^\circ=9\sqrt3$. The regular hexagon is made of $6$ equilateral triangles of side $6$, so its area is $6\cdot\frac{\sqrt3}{4}\cdot36=54\sqrt3$. Removing the three corner triangles leaves $54\sqrt3-3\cdot9\sqrt3=\boxed{27\sqrt3}$.

